window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] = window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] || {};
window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][125] = "<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> estimation · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C04 · <strong>Competency:</strong> TE · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Estimate the network capacity needed for a live stream with five million concurrent viewers.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;A lot of bandwidth, maybe a few hundred gigabits,&quot; with no calculation.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Multiplies viewers by bitrate, converts units correctly, adds headroom, and gives a server count.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Handles bits versus bytes, peak and ramp-up, total data volume and cost, and ties the numbers to a design decision such as a content network and bitrate tiers.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'll define the quantity as peak outbound bandwidth to viewers and the total data delivered for a two-hour event. Bandwidth. Five million concurrent viewers. With adaptive quality, many viewers receive a mid-tier stream, so I'll assume an average of 4 megabits per second. 5,000,000 × 4 Mbps = 20,000,000 Mbps = 20 terabits per second. In bytes, dividing by 8, that is 2.5 terabytes per second. I'm watching the units here: network capacity is in bits, storage and volume are in bytes. Volume. Two hours is 7,200 seconds. 2.5 TB/s × 7,200 = 18,000 TB = 18 petabytes. At an assumed delivery price of $0.01 per gigabyte (a judgment, and the kind of figure that changes with contracts <code>[as of 2026-10]</code>), 18 million gigabytes costs about $180,000. Capacity. If one edge server can send 40 gigabits per second, 20,000 Gbps ÷ 40 = 500 servers at full load. No one runs at 100%, so with a target of 50% utilization for headroom the fleet is about 1,000 servers, spread across regions because viewers are everywhere and a single site cannot carry the load. Check. Per viewer, 4 Mbps for two hours is 4 × 7,200 ÷ 8 = 3,600 megabytes, or 3.6 GB, and 5 million viewers × 3.6 GB = 18,000,000 GB, which matches the 18 petabytes. Both ways agree. Sensitivity and design. Bitrate dominates, so a lower top tier or a smarter ladder of quality levels cuts capacity directly: dropping the average from 4 to 3 Mbps saves 25%. The shape of the audience matters as much as the size: if viewers join within five minutes of the start, the peak is a ramp, and the system must be ready at the start, not on average. Because the ramp and the failure cost are what hurt, I'd ask about the distribution of start times and whether the stream can degrade gracefully (lower quality) rather than fail (C22 develops system-design answers).</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c04-07\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C04-07</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> estimation · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C04 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PR · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Your CEO says the market for our product is $10 billion. How would you test that in 15 minutes?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I'd research the market online and see if it's right.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Asks for the decomposition, rebuilds it bottom-up, finds the factor that differs, and gives a revised number.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Separates total from serviceable from obtainable, audits the claim for typical errors, gives a range, and says what to do with the gap.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd first ask where the number comes from, because a market claim is a decomposition in disguise. Suppose the claim is 5 million businesses times $2,000 a year, which is $10 billion. That is a clear structure, so I can test each factor. Factor one: 5 million businesses. Do all of them have the problem the product solves? I'd guess only about a fifth do, so 1 million. Factor two: can we reach them? A product sold through a certain channel reaches a share of them, say 60%, so 600,000. Factor three: the price. Is $2,000 what customers pay, or a list price for the highest tier? If typical buyers pay about $1,200, the serviceable market is 600,000 × $1,200 = $720 million. So the claim is about 14 times higher than my rebuild ($10 billion ÷ $0.72 billion). The gap comes from counting every business, counting them all as reachable and pricing them at the top tier. These are the usual errors: a total market presented as a serviceable one, and a price applied to buyers who would not pay it. Range. Taking each factor's plausible range (needing the product 10% to 30%, reachable 40% to 80%, price $800 to $1,800), the serviceable market is about 5 million × 0.10 × 0.40 × $800 = $160 million to 5 million × 0.30 × 0.80 × $1,800 = $2.16 billion, with a base of about $720 million. Even the high end is a fifth of the claim. What I'd do. I'd keep $10 billion as an upper bound on the total opportunity and use about $0.7 billion for planning, and I'd say which factor I'd test first: the share of businesses with the need, with ten customer interviews. I'd want to know what decision the number is being used for, because a $10 billion claim might be fine for a story to investors and wrong for a hiring plan.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c04-08\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C04-08</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> estimation · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C04 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PR · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> How many customer-support agents does a company need for a product with two million monthly users?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> Gives a number from instinct or a ratio of agents to users, with no decomposition.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Decomposes into contacts, handling time and agent hours, states the assumptions, gives a range and a check.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Chooses the swing assumption, converts the answer into cost per user, and says how deflection or peaks would change it.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I would build it from contacts and agent time. Two million monthly users. Suppose 8% contact support in a month (a judgment, anchored to products with good self-service): 160,000 contacts. Each takes about 12 minutes including notes: 160,000 × 12 ÷ 60 = 32,000 hours. An agent is paid for about 150 hours a month, but breaks, training and leave leave about 70% as handling time, so 105 hours: 32,000 ÷ 105 = about 305, so about 300 agents. Check. Two million users per 300 agents is one agent per 6,700 users, plausible for a product with good self-service and too lean for one with complex accounts. Cost: at $4,000 a month per agent, 300 agents cost $1.2 million, or $0.60 per user per month. Range. A contact rate of 5% gives 190 agents and 12% gives 457. So about 300, plausibly 190 to 460, and the contact rate is the swing assumption; I would take it from actual ticket counts. If the question is for a staffing plan, I would add that demand is not flat across the week.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c04-09\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C04-09</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> estimation · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C04 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PR · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> How many public electric-vehicle charging points does a city of three million people need in five years?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> Names a number or a ratio with no decomposition.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Estimates the stock of vehicles, the share that is electric and needs public charging, converts sessions into chargers, and gives a range.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Adds peak demand, identifies the swing assumption, and compares the result with a city further along instead of trusting it.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I would estimate a stock of vehicles first, then convert it into demand for chargers. Three million people. Suppose one car per four people: 750,000 cars. In five years suppose 10% are electric (a judgment; plausible range 5% to 20%): 75,000 vehicles. Suppose 70% can charge at home or work, so 30% rely on public charging: 22,500 vehicles. Each needs about two public sessions a month: 45,000 sessions a month, 1,500 a day. From sessions to chargers. A public charger handles about 8 sessions a day (about an hour each over eight busy hours, an assumption): 1,500 ÷ 8 = about 188. Demand clusters in evenings and at weekends, so I would plan for 1.5 times that: about 281 chargers. Check. 281 chargers for 75,000 electric vehicles is one per 267 vehicles. I would compare that with a city further along in adoption instead of trusting it. Range: electric share of 5% gives about 141 and 20% about 562, so about 281, plausibly 141 to 562. The electric share dominates, then the share who can charge at home. If this were for a procurement decision, I would stage the build against the adoption actually observed each year.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c04-10\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C04-10</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> estimation · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C04 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PR · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A designer says a change will save each user two seconds on a task they do many times a day. Is it worth building?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> Says two seconds is too small to matter, or that every improvement is worth it, without numbers.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Multiplies out the time saved, converts it to value, haircuts it for how time savings really behave, and compares with the cost.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Separates the customer's value from what the company can capture, says what else the change might move (friction, retention), and proposes a cheap way to test it.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I would turn two seconds into a total and then into a decision. Suppose a work tool with 200,000 daily active seats, each doing the task 25 times a day (invented). Time saved: 200,000 × 25 × 2 seconds = 10,000,000 seconds, about 2,778 hours a day, and over 250 working days about 694,444 hours a year. Value. At a loaded cost of $40 an hour that is about $27.8 million a year of customer time. But two-second savings are not recovered as work; people do not convert slivers into output. Suppose only 30% is realized: about $8.3 million. The company captures a small part through retention and willingness to pay; suppose 5%: about $416,667 a year. Cost. Suppose two engineers for three months plus design and testing: about $150,000. Payback is about 4.3 months on the captured value. So it clears the bar, but the number rests on two weak assumptions, the realized share and the captured share. What I would add. The larger effect of a faster task may be less friction, which shows up as more use or fewer abandoned tasks, not as saved time. I would test a faster version on a subset of seats and read task completion and weekly use, instead of arguing about the value of two seconds. I would change my mind if the task is rare for most users, because then the total shrinks.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c04-11\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C04-11</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> estimation · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C04 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PR · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> In a country with 800 million adults, estimate the payments per second a payments app must handle at its evening peak.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> Gives an average rate, or a number with no peak and no decomposition.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Decomposes users, payments per user and the daily total, converts to a rate, and applies a peak factor with a stated reason.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Separates the average from the peak minute, checks the ratio between them, gives a range, and says what the number means for how the system is sized.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I would define the unit first: a completed payment. Adults: 800 million (invented). Suppose 60% have a bank or wallet account: 480 million. On a typical day suppose 25% of them pay with the app: 120 million active users. Each makes about 2.5 payments: 300 million payments a day, which is 3,472 a second on average. Payments are not spread evenly. Suppose the busiest hour carries 15% of the day: 45 million an hour, 12,500 a second. The peak minute runs above the peak hour, say 1.5 times: about 18,750 a second. Check. The peak is about 5.4 times the average, plausible because payments cluster in a few evening hours and around paydays. A ratio near 1 would mean flat demand and one near 20 would suggest a spike-driven product such as ticketing. Per capita, 120 million daily users is 15% of all adults, which I would test against any published usage figure. Range. The share of account holders who pay on a given day dominates: 15% to 35% gives about 11,250 to 26,250 a second at the peak. So about 18,750, plausibly 11,000 to 26,000. What it means. The system must be sized for the peak minute, with headroom for festival or payday days, not for the average of 3,472; so the question to engineering is the capacity at the busiest minute and what degrades first when it is exceeded.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c05-01\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C05-01</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> metrics · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C05 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AN · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> What would you use as the success metric for a &quot;save for later&quot; feature in a news app?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Number of articles saved,&quot; with no link to value.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Starts from the goal, chooses a reading-based primary metric with a definition, and adds a guardrail.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Explains why saves alone are gameable, defines the denominator, adds a counter metric for unread clutter, and says how to learn whether the feature causes more reading.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The goal of save-for-later is that people read things they cared about but had no time for. The behavior that shows value is reading saved articles, not saving them, because saving costs a tap and does not mean anything was gained. So my primary metric is the saved-article read rate: of articles saved in a week, the share opened and read to at least 50% within seven days of saving. The numerator is saved articles read to 50% within seven days. The denominator is articles saved that week, excluding saves made by import or by a bulk tool. The unit is the saved article, and I'd report it by user as well, since a few heavy savers can dominate. Inputs: the share of weekly active readers who save at least one article, saves per saver, and the share of saved articles that get a reminder or reappear in a list. Guardrails: total reading time must not fall, which would suggest saving replaces reading now with reading never; and article load time must stay flat. My counter metric is the share of saved articles never opened after 30 days. If it climbs, the feature is a graveyard. Target: a read rate of 35% within eight weeks (assumed). To learn whether the feature causes more reading, I'd compare readers who see the save button with a holdout who do not, looking at articles read per week. I'd change my mind if saves rise and reads do not: the metric would show the feature is a habit of hoarding, and the fix would be reminders or surfacing saves at a better time.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c05-02\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C05-02</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> metrics · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C05 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AN · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> What is the difference between a leading and a lagging indicator? Give a product example and a way it can go wrong.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> Defines both loosely without a product example.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Gives clear definitions, an example pair, and why a team needs both.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Explains the need to validate the leading indicator's link to the outcome, gives a failure mode (a leading indicator that stops predicting, or is gamed), and says how to check.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> A lagging indicator reports a result after it has happened: quarterly churn, annual revenue, 90-day retention. It is reliable and slow, so by the time it moves it is hard to change. A leading indicator moves earlier and predicts the result: how many days a new user was active in the first week. A product pair: 90-day retention is the lagging indicator for a learning app, and the share of new users who complete three lessons in their first week is the leading indicator. The team can see the leading indicator in seven days and act on it, while the lagging one takes three months. A team needs both: the lagging indicator says whether we succeeded and the leading one says whether we are on track in time to change course. How it goes wrong. First, the link may not be real. If users who complete three lessons are simply the ones who would have stayed anyway, pushing more users to do it may not raise retention. I'd check by correlation over past cohorts and then by an experiment that increases first-week lessons and watches 90-day retention. Second, the leading indicator becomes a target and is gamed: making the first three lessons very short raises the share completing them and teaches nothing. A counter metric such as a short quiz on what was taught would show it. Third, the relationship drifts as the product or audience changes, so I'd recheck it each quarter.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c05-03\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C05-03</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> metrics · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C05 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AN · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Apple</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Define success metrics for a smartwatch sleep-tracking feature, including a privacy guardrail.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Number of people who use it and their sleep scores,&quot; with no guardrails.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Sets a goal, a primary usage metric, inputs, product guardrails (battery, accuracy) and a privacy guardrail.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Treats consent and on-device processing as part of the metric design, accounts for the bias from users who opt out of measurement, adds a counter metric for harm (anxiety about sleep scores), and avoids health claims it cannot support.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> <em>Setting only: no company data is used or implied.</em> The goal is that people understand their sleep and can choose to act on it, which requires that they wear the watch to bed regularly and look at the results. The feature is not a medical product in this answer, so I'd measure usage and perceived usefulness, not health outcomes I cannot validate. Primary metric: sustained overnight use, defined as the share of eligible watch owners who wear the watch to sleep on at least four nights a week for four consecutive weeks. Numerator: owners meeting that rule in a four-week window. Denominator: owners with the feature available and the sleep setting turned on at the start of the window. Inputs: setup completion, nights worn per week, and morning review (the share of worn nights followed by opening the summary). Product guardrails: battery life through a full day after a night of tracking must not fall below the current standard, since people who cannot charge in the morning will drop the feature, and the rate of nights with no data due to wear or charging problems should not rise. Counter metrics: the share of users who turn tracking off within 30 days (to see if it annoys), and the rate of reports or survey responses from people who say that sleep scores make them anxious about sleep, because a score can create the problem it measures. Privacy guardrail: sleep data is sensitive personal data. Where possible I'd compute usage metrics on the device or in aggregate, report only counts and rates, and collect nothing at the individual level without consent. The guardrail metrics are the opt-in rate for sharing analytics and a count of data-access incidents, which must be zero. The cost is measurement bias: the data only covers people who opted in, who may be more engaged than those who did not. I'd say that in the result, and I'd supplement with an in-product survey that does not depend on tracking, to see whether opt-in users differ. I'd change my plan if the nights-worn distribution shows a bimodal pattern: some people wear it nightly and many never, which would point to setup or comfort and not to the feature itself.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c05-04\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C05-04</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> metrics · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C05 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AN · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Netflix</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> How would you measure the success of a personalized recommendations row on a streaming service?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Click-through rate and hours watched.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Defines satisfying viewing as the goal, uses a funnel from impression to sustained viewing, and adds guardrails.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Warns that click and hours are gameable, adds a regret counter metric, considers diversity and long-term retention, and proposes a long-run holdout.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> <em>Setting only: no company data or internal metrics are claimed.</em> The goal of the row is to help a member find something they want to watch quickly and enjoy it, so that they keep subscribing. A click is not the goal: a misleading thumbnail raises clicks and lowers satisfaction. The funnel: impression of the row, play started from the row, and sustained viewing (watching at least 10 minutes, or finishing the title). Primary metric: satisfied plays per active member per week, defined as plays started from the row that reach 10 minutes, divided by active members in the week, excluding autoplay previews. The 10-minute threshold filters accidental starts, and it can be tuned by title length. Inputs: the row's play rate (plays per impression), the sustained-view rate (plays reaching 10 minutes), and time from opening the service to a satisfied play. Guardrails: total viewing per member should not fall (which would mean the row replaces better choices), and the diversity of titles played (share of plays outside the most popular ten percent of titles) should not collapse, so the row does not narrow taste. Counter metric: regret, defined as plays abandoned within two minutes, which a clickbait-style row would raise. The long-run measure is retention. Satisfied plays may lead retention by weeks or months, so I'd run a long-term holdout of members who see a simpler row, and compare their cancellation rate. Short tests would see plays, and only a holdout would see whether the row helps keep members. I'd change my view if satisfied plays rose and retention did not, which would mean the row shifts viewing within the service but does not add value.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c05-05\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C05-05</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> metrics · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C05 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AN · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> What metrics would you use to evaluate an AI writing assistant that suggests text as people type?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Number of users and number of suggestions.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Uses acceptance and edit behavior as the primary signal, adds quality, latency and cost guardrails.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Notes that acceptance can mean trust or inattention, adds an audit of accepted suggestions, links cost to acceptance, and separates offline quality evaluation from online metrics.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The goal is that people finish writing faster or better with suggestions they trust. The primary signal is suggestions accepted and kept: of suggestions shown, the share accepted and not substantially edited or deleted within a minute (the numerator is accepted suggestions that survive; the denominator is suggestions shown; the unit is the suggestion, reported also per user). Inputs: how often suggestions are shown relative to typing, acceptance rate, edit distance after acceptance, and the share of documents completed with the assistant on. Guardrails: latency of suggestions (the 95th percentile must be short enough to arrive before the user types past it), harmful or inappropriate suggestion rate, and cost per suggestion. A counter metric for over-acceptance: acceptance can rise when users stop reading carefully, so I'd audit a sample of accepted suggestions for errors and track the rate of later corrections. Cost links to the metric: if each suggestion costs about $0.004 and the acceptance rate is 25%, the cost per accepted suggestion is $0.004 ÷ 0.25 = $0.016. Raising the acceptance rate to 40% lowers it to $0.01, so quality improvements also lower the cost per accepted suggestion. Online metrics sit on top of an offline evaluation: before launch I'd score suggestions against a held-out set with human review, so I know quality before users see it (evals are covered in C24 and C25). I'd change my plan if acceptance is high but the audit finds frequent errors: the assistant would be trusted more than it deserves.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c05-06\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C05-06</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> metrics · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C05 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AN · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Meta</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Time spent per user on your social app is up 8% after a ranking change. Is that good?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Yes, more time spent is more engagement.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Asks what changed, decomposes the increase, checks quality and counter metrics, and gives a conditional answer.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Challenges time spent as a proxy for value, separates active from passive time, checks retention and well-being signals, considers the second-order effects, and says what would make it good.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> <em>Setting only: no company data is used.</em> I'd say &quot;it depends on where the time comes from,&quot; and then find out. First, clarify the metric: is it total minutes per daily user, and how is time measured (foreground time, or including time with the app idle)? What exactly changed in ranking, and when? Second, decompose. Time per user = sessions per user × minutes per session. And split the time by kind: interacting with people (commenting, messaging) versus passive consumption (scrolling, watching). If the 8% is almost all passive video, it is a different story from an 8% rise in conversations. Third, check quality. Look at next-day and 30-day retention of the exposed group compared with a holdout; at reports and &quot;not interested&quot; actions; at notification opt-outs; and at survey measures of whether people feel their time was well spent. If retention is flat or lower and negative feedback is up, the time is a short-term effect. Fourth, the second-order effects. Rankings that raise time spent often favor sensational content, and over time users who feel worse leave or reduce use. Advertisers may buy fewer impressions of poor quality. So: good if the extra time is in meaningful interaction, retention and satisfaction hold, and negative feedback does not rise; a warning if it comes from passive, high-arousal content. As a documented example of a company choosing to move this metric down on purpose, CS-C05-02 describes Facebook's 2018 decision. I'd propose making &quot;time spent in interactions that users later say they valued&quot; the primary measure for this change, with time spent as an input, and I'd run the test with a long-term holdout. I'd change my view if the holdout shows higher 90-day retention for the exposed group.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c05-07\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C05-07</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> metrics · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C05 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AN · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Choose a north star metric for a two-sided marketplace that connects freelancers with clients, and defend it.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Gross revenue&quot; or &quot;number of users.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names candidates, tests them against criteria, chooses one with a definition, and adds inputs and guardrails for both sides.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Addresses quality and liquidity, shows why the obvious candidates fail, defines the metric precisely, handles the two sides separately, and says what would change the choice as the marketplace matures.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The value of this marketplace is a good match: a client gets work done well and a freelancer gets paid for it. Candidates: 1. Gross merchandise value (total paid): fails the value test because it rises with higher prices or longer projects, and fails the gaming test. 2. Number of registered users: a vanity metric that rises by construction. 3. Contracts started per week: better, but a contract that ends badly counts as a success. 4. Successful contracts per week: contracts that were completed and rated satisfactory by both sides, counted in the week they completed. I choose the fourth. Definition: a contract is successful if the work was delivered, the client released payment without a dispute, and the client rated it at least four of five (an assumed threshold). The numerator is successful contracts completed in the week, and the unit is the contract. It passes the tests: it reflects value for both sides, the team can move it through matching and trust features, it moves in weeks, and it is hard to inflate without genuine completion. Inputs, by side: demand (clients posting jobs, jobs receiving qualified proposals, time to first proposal), supply (freelancers active, proposals per job), and match quality (share of jobs filled, hire rate). Guardrails: dispute rate, freelancer earnings per hour, client repeat rate, and the share of payments made through the platform (to detect leakage). Counter metric: the rate of jobs filled by a freelancer with very few ratings, which a push for fill rate might raise at the expense of quality. I'd revisit as the marketplace matures: when most jobs are filled, the constraint shifts from liquidity to quality, and a measure of repeat contracts between the same pair would carry more weight. I'd also watch for a gap between sides: a metric that rises because clients are well served while freelancers earn less would be a warning.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c05-08\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C05-08</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> metrics · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C05 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AN · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A support team's quarterly goal is &quot;tickets closed.&quot; You suspect the number is being gamed. What do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Tell the team to stop gaming and be honest.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Looks for evidence of gaming, proposes counter metrics, and redefines the goal.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Treats gaming as a system response not a character flaw, proposes a metric set that makes the cheap path visible, handles the change with the team, and states what outcome the team should actually be measured on.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd start from the assumption that people are responding sensibly to what they are measured on, so the question is what the metric rewards. First, test the suspicion with data, not accusation. Look for signs: closure times that cluster just before a deadline; tickets closed and reopened; a rise in the share of tickets closed as &quot;resolved&quot; with no reply; a growing number of repeat contacts about the same issue; closure counts rising while customer satisfaction is flat or falling. If I see repeat contact up and satisfaction down, gaming is likely. Second, find what &quot;tickets closed&quot; rewards. It rewards closing easy tickets first and closing hard ones quickly, whether or not they were resolved. A <a href=\"#gl-second-order-effect\">second-order effect</a> is that hard tickets age and customers contact again. Third, redesign the metric set. The goal should be customers whose problem is solved. A primary metric could be the resolution rate: tickets resolved without a repeat contact within seven days, divided by tickets closed. Counters: customer satisfaction on resolved tickets, and the age of the oldest open tickets (to stop easy-first cherry-picking). Keep closed tickets as an input (volume), not the target. Fourth, handle it with the team. I'd show them the data about repeat contacts, explain that the old target made the bad behavior rational, and agree the new set with their input. I'd also audit a sample of closures by hand for the first two months, and I'd avoid punishing the old behavior, since the old target created it. I'd change my approach if the review shows no gaming: then the question is whether closed tickets is the right measure anyway, and the answer is probably still to add a quality counter.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c06-01\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C06-01</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C06 · <strong>Competency:</strong> DI · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Customers keep asking us to add a &quot;bulk edit&quot; feature to our project-management tool. What do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Build it, since customers are asking,&quot; or &quot;ask customers what they want.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Steps up from the request to the job, checks who is asking, weighs the evidence, and offers more than one solution.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Separates segments among the requesters, uses behavior as well as requests, proposes the cheapest test, and says what would make the answer no.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd treat &quot;bulk edit&quot; as a solution and look for the problem under it. I'd ask the people who requested it to walk me through the last time they needed to change many items at once: what were they doing, how many items, how often, what did they do instead? Common workarounds are exporting to a spreadsheet and re-importing, or editing items one by one. Then I'd check who is asking. If the requests come mostly from team leads managing hundreds of tasks, it is a different segment from a casual user with a dozen tasks. I'd look at behavior: how many users perform repeated single edits in a row (a signal that bulk edit would help), and what share of tickets or sales notes mention it. Suppose the interviews show the real job is &quot;after a planning meeting, I want to move twenty tasks to a new sprint and reassign owners, so I can leave with everything in order.&quot; Bulk edit is one solution. Others are a &quot;move a group&quot; action, a planning view that allows drag and drop, or a template for sprint planning. They differ in cost, and the narrower ones may serve the job. My recommendation is to ship the narrowest version that serves the job for the team-lead segment, and test it with five customers who asked, with the criterion that at least four use it in their next planning meeting. The cost is that other bulk operations wait. I'd change my mind if the behavior data show that few users perform repeated edits, in which case the requests are loud but not widespread.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c06-02\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C06-02</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C06 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A language-learning app serves students, working professionals and retirees. Which segment would you build for first?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Students, since there are the most of them,&quot; with no criterion.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Segments by need, scores on stated criteria, chooses one, and names what it gives up.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Notes the segments may be defined by a job (not an age), tests the choice with evidence, and says what would change it.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'll first note that age is a weak guide to need. What matters is why each person is learning, so I'd restate the segments by job: students learning for exams, professionals learning for a move or a role abroad, and retirees learning for travel and enjoyment. I'll score them on size, pain intensity, reach and our right to win (FW-C06-02), with rough assumed values. | Segment (by job) | Size | Pain intensity | Reach | Fit and right to win | |---|---|---|---|---| | Exam learners | High | Medium (deadline, but school provides structure) | Medium (crowded channels) | Medium | | Professionals relocating | Medium | High (a date, real stakes, willingness to pay) | High (can reach through employers and expat forums) | High (needs practical speaking, which the product can deliver) | | Retirees learning for travel | Medium | Low to medium (no deadline) | Low (different channels) | Medium | I choose professionals who are relocating. The deciding criterion is pain intensity combined with reach: they have a deadline and they pay, and we can reach them directly. I give up the size of the exam-learner segment and the long-term loyalty of retirees. Evidence I'd collect to confirm: interviews with eight people who relocated in the past year about how they prepared, which tools they used and what failed; the share who paid for tutoring; and a landing-page test for a &quot;speak at work in 30 days&quot; offer. I'd change my choice if interviews showed that relocating professionals mostly rely on employer-provided classes, which would shrink our opening.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c06-03\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C06-03</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C06 · <strong>Competency:</strong> DI · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> People don't use our calendar app's scheduling assistant. How would you find out why?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Survey users to ask why they don't use it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Segments non-users, lists hypotheses, combines behavioral data, interviews and a survey, and defines the decision.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Separates &quot;never saw it,&quot; &quot;tried and left&quot; and &quot;doesn't need it,&quot; sets a decision rule, and addresses sampling bias against people who have already left.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> First the decision: do we invest in the assistant, change how it is discovered, or retire it? I'd also define &quot;don't use&quot;: say 4% of weekly users have used it in the last month (assumed), against an expectation of 15%. The non-users are three different groups, and the research should separate them: people who never saw the assistant (discovery), people who tried it and stopped (a usability or value problem), and people who looked and decided they don't need it (a need problem). Each leads to a different action. Behavioral data comes first and is cheap: what share of weekly users saw the entry point, what share of those started it, what share of starters completed a scheduled meeting, and what share used it again. Say exposure is 20%, start rate 30% of those, completion 50% of starters. Then a user who sees it has a 15% chance of finishing once (0.30 × 0.50), and only 20% see it, so overall 3% finish (0.20 × 0.15), close to the observed 4%. The biggest loss is exposure. Interviews come second: eight people from each group, asking them to describe the last time they scheduled a meeting with several people, how they did it, and what they tried. For the &quot;tried and left&quot; group, I'd ask them to show me what happened. A short survey third, built from the interviews, would size how common each reason is. Sampling is the danger: people who left the app are not in my active-user list, so I'd recruit some lapsed users separately. My decision rule: if the main loss is exposure and those who try it complete meetings, fix discovery; if many who try it abandon, fix the experience; if most say they don't schedule multi-person meetings, retire it. I'd change the plan if the need turns out to be concentrated in a segment such as team admins, and I'd focus on them.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c06-04\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C06-04</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C06 · <strong>Competency:</strong> DI · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> In a survey, 35% of customers say they would pay $15 a month for a new feature. Do you believe it?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Yes, 35% is a strong signal,&quot; or &quot;No, surveys are unreliable.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names the say-do gap, computes the margin of error, asks about sampling, and proposes a behavioral test.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Estimates a discount factor, tests with a commitment, converts the result into a revenue range, and says what would change the plan.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd treat the 35% as the upper bound of interest, not a forecast, for three reasons. First, it is a stated intention about future behavior, which is where the say-do gap is largest. People say yes to hypothetical purchases because saying yes costs nothing. Second, sampling: how many responded and who? Suppose 300 responded out of 2,000 invited (assumed), a 15% response rate, so the respondents are more interested than the typical customer. The margin of error on 35% from 300 responses is about 1.96 × the square root of (0.35 × 0.65 ÷ 300) = about 5.4 points, so the survey says 30% to 40% of respondents, before any non-response bias. Third, price and framing: how was the feature described, and was the price shown at the moment of choice? To get a behavioral number, I'd run a commitment test: show the offer with a real checkout to a sample, or take refundable pre-orders. Suppose 5,000 users see the offer (assumed) and 1.5% pay. Then the working conversion is about 1.5%, and the stated-to-actual ratio is about 23 (35% ÷ 1.5%). I'd use that ratio, labeled as specific to this audience and offer, to discount future surveys. For 50,000 active customers (assumed), 1.5% is 750 payers, at $15 a month that is $135,000 a year. The survey implied 17,500 payers, or $3.15 million a year, so the forecast error would have been more than twenty-fold. I'd decide on the behavioral number, and I'd say what would change the plan: a price test or a different packaging that raises conversion above about 4%, which is the level at which the feature covers a small team (assumed).</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c06-05\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C06-05</h3>\n";
